<description>
Erase each foreground object that contains more than one sparse marker-color pixel.

Input structure
- A dominant background surrounds separated 4-connected foreground objects.
- The foreground uses one dense object color and one sparse marker color.
Rule
- Infer the marker as the unique less frequent foreground color.
- Keep objects with zero or one marker pixel.
- Recolor every pixel of an object to background when it contains two or more marker pixels.
Key insight
- The marker count inside each whole object, not the object's size or position, decides whether that object remains.
</description>
<annotation>
{
  "type": "transform_in_place",
  "src": "4-connected non-bg objects; marker color is the unique less frequent foreground color",
  "transform": "erase to bg each object with two or more marker-color pixels; preserve objects with zero or one marker pixel"
}
</annotation>
<generation_spec>
```yaml
spec:
  grid: 13-14 rows x 12-15 cols
  colors:
    bg: 0 [observed]
    dense: 1 [observed]
    marker: 8 [observed]
  objects:
    count: 6-7 solid rectangles (100% fill)
    height: 2-4
    width: 2-6
    pixels: each cell is dense or marker color
    marker_per_object: 0-4; kept objects have 0 or 1, removed have 2-4
    separation: min Chebyshev distance >= 2 between any two objects
    may_touch_border: true
  transform:
    rule: erase (set to bg) every object containing >= 2 marker pixels
    kept_count: 3 [observed]
    removed_count: 3-4
    both_kept_and_removed: required
  totals:
    marker_pixels: 9-14
    dense_to_marker_ratio: 4-8x [observed]

generation_strategy:
  approach: direct construction (simple placement task)
  steps:
    1. Pick grid size in range 13-14 x 12-15.
    2. Decide object count (6-7). For each, pick random rectangle dims h in [2,4], w in [2,6].
    3. Assign each object a marker count -- 3 objects get 0 or 1 (kept), rest get 2-4 (removed).
    4. Place rectangles on grid with Chebyshev gap >= 2 between all pairs. Use greedy random placement with rejection.
    5. Fill each rectangle with dense color, then randomly replace marker_count cells with marker color.
    6. Verify total marker pixels in 9-14 and dense/marker ratio reasonable; retry assignment if not.
```
</generation_spec>
